@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

Fjalar is built as a tool on top of the Valgrind instrumentation framework.
It incorporates code from the Valgrind tool Memcheck.  When new
versions of Valgrind and Memcheck are released, Fjalar (and Kvasir)
should be updated
to incorporate the Valgrind/Memcheck changes. This document tells you
how to do so.

Conceptually, this is two separate updates: (1) Updating our underlying
copy of Valgrind to the newer version of Valgrind, and (2) Updating the
Memcheck code contained in Fjalar to the newer version of Memcheck. Due
to very tight dependence of the Memcheck code on Valgrind, the changes
should be done simultaneously.

The update of the underlying copy of Valgrind can usually be done almost
automatically. Updating the Memcheck code often involves manual work,
due to substantial modifications to the Memcheck code that are incorporated
in Fjalar. The more frequent the merges are, the easier they will be to
do. This document gives instructions for merging only when an official
release is made.  This has the advantage of ensuring that the tool is more
stable, but the disadvantages of going longer between bug fixes and making
each merge larger and more difficult.

Fjalar also contains code derived from the GNU Binutils
tool @command{readelf}.  @xref{Updating Readelf from Binutils}
to update Fjalar when a new version of Binutils is released.

@menu
* Determining whether a merge is necessary::
@end menu


@node Determining whether a merge is necessary
@section Determining whether a merge is necessary

To determine whether a newer version of Valgrind is available, compare file
@url{https://raw.githubusercontent.com/codespecs/fjalar/master/valgrind/REVISION,
@file{fjalar/valgrind/REVISION}} to URL
@url{https://valgrind.org/downloads/current.html}.

To determine whether a newer version of Binutils is available, compare file
@url{https://raw.githubusercontent.com/codespecs/fjalar/master/valgrind/REVISION,
@file{fjalar/valgrind/REVISION}} to URL
@url{https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/binutils/?C=M;O=D}.


@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started

The instructions in this document assume you use bash as your shell.
If you use a different shell, you will need to adjust the instructions
accordingly.  Also, we assume @env{$INV} points to a parent directory that
contains both the Daikon and Fjalar source trees, and that @env{$DAIKONDIR}
points to the Daikon source tree within it:

@example
export DAIKONDIR=$INV/daikon
@end example

If you already had @env{$DAIKONDIR} set, make sure it is @file{$INV/daikon}.
If it pointed elsewhere, some steps would build and test one copy of Daikon,
and other steps would use a different copy.

@menu
* Obtain a copy of Daikon and Fjalar::
* Ensure your source is up to date::
* Verify current sources pass the regression tests::
* Get Valgrind version tags::
* Obtain a copy of the base Valgrind source tree::
* Obtain a copy of the target Valgrind source tree::
* Correct the files of earlier merges::
@end menu


@node Obtain a copy of Daikon and Fjalar
@section Obtain a copy of Daikon and Fjalar

(You may skip this step if you already have local clones of these repositories.)

Clone a copy of Daikon and Fjalar from our GitHub
repositories:

@example
export INV=`pwd`
export DAIKONDIR=$INV/daikon
git clone https://github.com/codespecs/daikon.git
git clone https://github.com/codespecs/fjalar.git
@end example

@node Ensure your source is up to date
@section Ensure your source is up to date

(If you just made fresh clones of the repositories, you may skip this step.)

Update your enlistment to the latest versions:

@example
git -C $DAIKONDIR pull
git -C $INV/fjalar pull
@end example

Ensure there are no local changes:

@example
git -C $DAIKONDIR diff
git -C $INV/fjalar diff
@end example

The diff commands should produce no output. If there are any differences,
commit and push them, then start over from the beginning.

Ensure there are no outgoing changes:

@example
git -C $DAIKONDIR log --branches --not --remotes
git -C $INV/fjalar log --branches --not --remotes
@end example

The log commands should produce no output. If there are outgoing changes,
push them, then start over from the beginning.


@node Verify current sources pass the regression tests
@section Verify current sources pass the regression tests

Re-compile Fjalar, then run Daikon's Kvasir tests. (The tests take about 20 minutes.)

@example
# Optional, to verify Daikon
make -C $DAIKONDIR compile test

make -C $INV/fjalar build
make -C $DAIKONDIR/tests/kvasir-tests clean-all
make -C $DAIKONDIR/tests/kvasir-tests regression-tests
@end example

The Kvasir tests use the Fjalar installed under
@file{$DAIKONDIR/fjalar/valgrind/inst}; they build it only if it is not
already installed.  If @file{$DAIKONDIR/fjalar} is not
@file{$INV/fjalar} (or a symbolic link to it), they test a different copy of Fjalar than the one you are merging; check that
@code{readlink -f $DAIKONDIR/fjalar} and @code{readlink -f $INV/fjalar}
print the same directory.  Alternatively,
@command{$INV/fjalar/test-kvasir.sh} runs Daikon's Kvasir and DynComp
tests against @file{$INV/fjalar}.  It uses the Daikon in
@file{$DAIKONDIR}, cloning Daikon there if that directory does not
exist, and points @file{$DAIKONDIR/fjalar} at @file{$INV/fjalar}.  If
@file{$DAIKONDIR} already existed, the script restores
@file{$DAIKONDIR/fjalar} when it exits; if the script cloned Daikon, it
leaves the link in place.  It refuses to run if @file{$DAIKONDIR/fjalar}
exists and is not a symbolic link.  In that case, set
@env{DAIKONDIR} for that one command only (do not @code{export} it,
because later steps use your usual @env{DAIKONDIR}):

@example
DAIKONDIR=/tmp/$USER/daikon-test-kvasir $INV/fjalar/test-kvasir.sh
@end example

The tests should pass. If any tests fail, fix them, then start over from
the beginning.
Continuous integration (GitHub Actions, configured in
@file{.github/workflows/ci.yml}) runs these tests on Ubuntu and Rocky Linux;
see that file for the exact operating system versions.

For a Valgrind update, also run Valgrind's @command{regtest} tests on the
current sources, and save the failures that they observe, outside the
Fjalar tree:

@example
$INV/fjalar/check-regtest.sh
cp $INV/fjalar/regtest-<os>-observed.txt $INV/regtest-before-merge.txt
@end example

@noindent
where @file{regtest-<os>.log} is the log that @command{check-regtest.sh}
names when it starts, such as @file{regtest-ubuntu-26.04.log}.
@command{check-regtest.sh} must report no
unexpected failures; see
@ref{Get Current Valgrind Regtest Baseline Results}, which also says what
to do if this operating system has no expected-failures file.
@ref{Testing Valgrind} compares the failures after the merge with
@file{regtest-before-merge.txt}.  It cannot use
@file{regtest-expected/<os>.txt} for that, because that file may list
tests that no longer fail.

At this point, the source trees are ready for the merge.
Remove the build products, including the files that
@command{automake} and @command{autoconf} generated, so that the build
after the merge starts from scratch, with no files left over from the
base Valgrind release:

@example
make -C $INV/fjalar very-clean
@end example

The merge stops rather than overwrite a file that Git does not track and
that @file{.gitignore} does not match.  The following command lists such
files.  Review the list.  In most cases, you will want to delete the
files; commit any that Fjalar should have.

@example
git -C $INV/fjalar ls-files --others --exclude-standard
@end example


@node Get Valgrind version tags
@section Get Valgrind version tags

Execute the following Git command:

@smallexample
git ls-remote --tags --refs https://sourceware.org/git/valgrind.git | grep -v svn | sort -k2V
@end smallexample

@noindent
The output will list the tags of the most recent releases of Valgrind.  It will
look something like:

@example
4b818ef7a776a9a003b5ddaf6f4285caa90e6e47        refs/tags/VALGRIND_3_14_0
223b0c579b4c31d1cf3c1975a1ded9821bbf7279        refs/tags/VALGRIND_3_15_0
@end example

Assuming that the Valgrind merge process has been done on a regular basis,
the last @samp{tags} line will show the tag of the next (target) version and
the second to last @samp{tags} line will show the tag of the current
version.  You should verify the current one by comparing it with the
version number in the file @file{valgrind/REVISION}.  Set two environment
variables to these tags, for use in the steps below.

@example
export VOLDTAG=VALGRIND_3_14_0
export VNEWTAG=VALGRIND_3_15_0
@end example


@node Obtain a copy of the base Valgrind source tree
@section Obtain a copy of the base Valgrind source tree

First, make the directory @file{valgrind-old} that contains a copy of the Valgrind
source tree that was used the last time a Valgrind merge was performed.

If you performed the last merge and saved the
@file{valgrind-old} and @file{valgrind-new} directories, then just run:

@smallexample
rm -rf $INV/valgrind-old
mv $INV/valgrind-new $INV/valgrind-old
git -C $INV/valgrind-old reset --hard && git -C $INV/valgrind-old clean -fdx
@end smallexample

@noindent
The @command{git reset} command discards any changes to files in the
repository, and the @command{git clean} command removes any build products.
@file{merge-valgrind.sh} refuses to run if @file{valgrind-old} or
@file{valgrind-new} has changes to its files.

Otherwise, run the following commands.

@smallexample
git clone -b $VOLDTAG --depth 1 https://sourceware.org/git/valgrind.git $INV/valgrind-old
@end smallexample


@node Obtain a copy of the target Valgrind source tree
@section Obtain a copy of the target Valgrind source tree

Next, make the directory @file{valgrind-new} that contains a copy of the
latest Valgrind source tree.

@smallexample
git clone -b $VNEWTAG --depth 1 https://sourceware.org/git/valgrind.git $INV/valgrind-new
@end smallexample

@c For a  full repro of the current Valgrind source:
@c git clone https://sourceware.org/git/valgrind.git valgrind-git

@node Correct the files of earlier merges
@section Correct the files of earlier merges

The merge adds and removes the files that the Valgrind developers
added and removed between the base and the target Valgrind releases.
It does not correct the files that earlier merges failed to add or
remove.

The following command lists them:

@example
$INV/fjalar/list-file-discrepancies.sh $INV/valgrind-old
@end example

@noindent
It prints five lists, each preceded by a heading line that starts with
@samp{#}:

@enumerate
@item
Files that Fjalar tracks but the base Valgrind release does not have,
which earlier merges failed to remove.
Review the list, because Fjalar may have added some of the files
on purpose.  Write the files that Fjalar should not have to a file
@file{$INV/files-to-remove.txt}, one per line, and remove them:

@example
xargs -r -d '\n' git -C $INV/fjalar rm -- < $INV/files-to-remove.txt
@end example

@item
Memcheck files that Fjalar's copy of Memcheck, in @file{valgrind/fjalar},
has but that the base Valgrind release does not have.  These are the files
in @file{valgrind/fjalar} whose names start with @file{mc_} or
@file{memcheck}; the other files there are Fjalar's own.
Fjalar created some of them, such as @file{mc_fjalar.h} and
@file{mc_translate.h}; keep those.
Remove the others with @command{git rm}.

@item
Memcheck files that the base Valgrind release has but that
@file{valgrind/fjalar} lacks.  These are the files that
@file{merge-valgrind.sh} merges into @file{valgrind/fjalar}:  all of
@file{memcheck} except @file{docs}, @file{tests}, and @file{Makefile.am}.
Fjalar builds every Memcheck source file, so its copy of Memcheck must
have every one.
Copy each of them from @file{$INV/valgrind-old/memcheck} and add it with
@command{git add}.

@item
Files that the base Valgrind release has but that Fjalar does not track,
which earlier merges failed to add.
Examples are empty files and files that match @file{.gitignore}, such
as @file{Makefile} or @file{*.log} files.
Unlike the other lists, these file names are relative to
@file{valgrind-old}, not to @file{$INV/fjalar}.
Review the list, because Fjalar may omit some Valgrind files on purpose.
Write the files that Fjalar should have to a file @file{$INV/files-to-add.txt},
one per line, and add them:

@smallexample
tar -C $INV/valgrind-old -cf - --verbatim-files-from -T $INV/files-to-add.txt \
  | tar -C $INV/fjalar/valgrind -xf -
sed 's|^|valgrind/|' $INV/files-to-add.txt | xargs -r -d '\n' git -C $INV/fjalar add -f --
@end smallexample

@item
Files whose mode in Fjalar differs from their mode in the base Valgrind
release, such as a test filter script that lacks its execute bit, or a
regular file that is a symbolic link in Valgrind.  Each line starts with
the file's mode in the base Valgrind release:  @samp{100644} for a
regular file, @samp{100755} for an executable file, or @samp{120000} for
a symbolic link.  The merge treats such a difference as a Fjalar change
and keeps it.  Review the list, because Fjalar may have changed some modes
on purpose.  Write the lines for the files whose mode should match the base
Valgrind release to a file @file{$INV/modes-to-fix.txt}, and change the
modes:

@smallexample
while read -r mode f; do
  case $mode in
    100644) chmod -x "$INV/fjalar/$f" ;;
    100755) chmod +x "$INV/fjalar/$f" ;;
    120000) ln -sfn "$(readlink "$INV/valgrind-old/$@{f#valgrind/@}")" "$INV/fjalar/$f" ;;
  esac
  git -C $INV/fjalar add -- "$f"
done < $INV/modes-to-fix.txt
@end smallexample
@end enumerate

Commit these corrections before you merge, because
@file{merge-valgrind.sh} requires that Fjalar has no uncommitted
changes.  First check that @command{git status} lists no untracked file
that you meant to add, then review and commit the corrections:

@example
git -C $INV/fjalar status
git -C $INV/fjalar diff --cached --stat
git -C $INV/fjalar commit -m "Correct the files of earlier Valgrind merges"
@end example

After the merge, the same command with @file{valgrind-new} in place of
@file{valgrind-old} lists the files that Fjalar tracks but the target
Valgrind release does not have, and vice versa (@pxref{Resolving Conflicts}).

