@node Merging Valgrind
@section Merging Valgrind

Fjalar contains two copies of Memcheck:  an unmodified one in
@file{valgrind/memcheck}, and a modified one (the @file{mc_*} files and
@file{memcheck.h}) in @file{valgrind/fjalar}.  We call the rest of Valgrind
``Coregrind''.  Our changes to Memcheck are much more substantial than our
changes to Coregrind.

Run:

@example
$INV/fjalar/merge-valgrind.sh
@end example

@noindent
The script merges the changes that the Valgrind developers made between
the base and the target Valgrind releases into Coregrind and into both
copies of Memcheck.  It creates two commits, which contain only
Valgrind's files:  @code{valgrind-merge/base} from
@file{$INV/valgrind-old}, and @code{valgrind-merge/new} from
@file{$INV/valgrind-new}.  In each, Valgrind is in @file{valgrind}, as in
Fjalar, and Valgrind's Memcheck is also in @file{valgrind/fjalar}, without
its @file{docs} and @file{tests} directories.  The script also records
Fjalar before the merge as @code{valgrind-merge/fjalar}, which stays the
same even if you commit during the merge.  Then the script runs
@command{git cherry-pick} of @code{valgrind-merge/new}.  That is a
3-way merge:  it compares the Fjalar version of each file, the base
Valgrind version, and the target Valgrind version.  It applies a change
even if Fjalar has changed the lines around it, and it leaves conflict
markers in a file where both Valgrind and Fjalar changed the same lines.
It also adds, removes, and renames files.  When the Valgrind developers
rename a file that Fjalar has changed, the merge moves Fjalar's changes to
the new name.  The merge leaves unchanged the files that only Fjalar has.

The script merges into the Fjalar tree that contains it, so run the copy
in the tree that you are merging into.  It requires that the tree has no
uncommitted changes, so that @command{git reset --hard} can undo the
merge.  It stages the result of the merge, but does not commit it.

These commands show what the Valgrind developers changed, in Coregrind
and in Memcheck:

@smallexample
git -C $INV/fjalar diff valgrind-merge/base valgrind-merge/new -- valgrind ':!valgrind/fjalar'
git -C $INV/fjalar diff valgrind-merge/base valgrind-merge/new -- valgrind/fjalar
@end smallexample

@noindent
These commands show Fjalar's changes to the base Valgrind, in Coregrind
and in Memcheck, before the merge:

@smallexample
git -C $INV/fjalar diff --diff-filter=a valgrind-merge/base valgrind-merge/fjalar -- valgrind ':!valgrind/fjalar'
git -C $INV/fjalar diff --diff-filter=a valgrind-merge/base valgrind-merge/fjalar -- valgrind/fjalar
@end smallexample

The merge does not change Fjalar's build file
@file{valgrind/fjalar/Makefile.am}, because @code{valgrind-merge/base}
and @code{valgrind-merge/new} omit it.  Instead, @file{merge-valgrind.sh}
runs @file{generate-fjalar-makefile.sh}, which regenerates it by
renaming Memcheck to Fjalar in @file{valgrind/memcheck/Makefile.am}
(which the merge updated).  Fjalar builds every Memcheck source file.
When the Valgrind developers add a source file to Memcheck, they list it
in @file{memcheck/Makefile.am}; the generated file lists it too, so
Fjalar compiles it without any change to @file{fjalar.am}.  The generated
file includes @file{valgrind/fjalar/fjalar.am}, which holds Fjalar's
changes, such as its additional source files and compiler flags.  Review
@command{git diff --cached valgrind/fjalar/Makefile.am}, and update
@file{fjalar.am} if an upstream change conflicts with Fjalar's changes.
The scripts list any line of the generated file that still mentions
Memcheck or docs, or that is a conflict marker.  Such a line may mean that
the renaming in @file{generate-fjalar-makefile.sh} needs to handle a new
upstream construct, or that @file{valgrind/memcheck/Makefile.am} has a
conflict.  In that case, or if @file{generate-fjalar-makefile.sh} fails,
@file{merge-valgrind.sh} does not stage
@file{valgrind/fjalar/Makefile.am}.  After resolving the conflict or
changing @file{generate-fjalar-makefile.sh} or @file{fjalar.am},
regenerate the file and stage it:

@example
$INV/fjalar/generate-fjalar-makefile.sh
git -C $INV/fjalar add valgrind/fjalar/Makefile.am
@end example


@node Resolving Conflicts
@section Resolving Conflicts

There may be a few conflicts --- this is OK.  If there are massive
numbers of conflicts or some other indication of failure, check that
@file{valgrind-old} is the Valgrind release that the last merge
merged into Fjalar.

@file{merge-valgrind.sh} lists the files with conflicts, and
@command{git status} lists them as unmerged.  Each conflict in a file is
marked by @samp{<<<<<<<}, @samp{|||||||}, @samp{=======}, and
@samp{>>>>>>>} lines, which surround Fjalar's version, the base Valgrind
version, and the target Valgrind version.  (The base Valgrind version is
omitted if your Git configuration sets @code{merge.conflictStyle} to
@samp{merge}.)  For a binary file, the Fjalar version
is left in place.  A file that the Valgrind developers removed but that
Fjalar has changed, or that Fjalar removed but the Valgrind developers
have changed, is a ``modify/delete'' conflict, and the merge leaves the
existing version in place.  It may help to refer to Fjalar's changes
to the base Valgrind during this process.  Mark each file as resolved with @command{git add} or
@command{git rm}, or use @command{git mergetool}.  When you are done, this
command produces no output:

@example
git -C $INV/fjalar diff --name-only --diff-filter=U
@end example

A merge can lose one of Fjalar's changes without reporting a conflict, and
so can a conflict resolution.  This command checks that the merge kept
each of Fjalar's changes:

@example
$INV/fjalar/compare-fjalar-changes.sh
@end example

@noindent
It compares Fjalar's changes to the base Valgrind release before the merge
with Fjalar's staged changes to the target Valgrind release, and prints
the differences between them as a diff of the two diffs, in which each
line is indented by four spaces.  After the indentation, a line that
starts with @samp{-} followed by @samp{+} or @samp{-} is part of a change
that was lost or altered, and a line that starts with @samp{+} followed by
@samp{+} or @samp{-} is part of a change that was added.  Review each
difference.  Some are expected:  a change that the Valgrind developers
also made is no longer one of Fjalar's changes, and a change that you
adapted to the Valgrind developers' changes differs.  For a file that the
Valgrind developers removed but that you kept, the command compares with
the base Valgrind's version of the file.  Run the command again after you
change a file and stage it.  The command exits with status 0 if there are
no differences, 1 if there are differences, and 2 if an error occurs.

A file that was removed upstream but that has Fjalar changes is not
removed; decide whether to remove it with @command{git rm}.  The Valgrind
developers probably moved the code of such a file to another file.
Before you remove the file, save Fjalar's changes to it, and then make
them in that other file.  These commands show Fjalar's changes from
before the merge; set
@env{f} to the name of the file, such as @file{valgrind/fjalar/mc_main.c}:

@example
f=valgrind/fjalar/mc_main.c
git -C $INV/fjalar diff valgrind-merge/base valgrind-merge/fjalar -- $f
@end example

Then check that Fjalar tracks no other file that the target Valgrind
release does not have, and that it has every file that the target
Valgrind release has:

@example
$INV/fjalar/list-file-discrepancies.sh $INV/valgrind-new
@end example

@noindent
Each list should contain only the files that Fjalar created and the
files that you decided to keep or to omit.

The largest modification to Memcheck is in @file{mc_translate.c} and special
care should be made to ensure it is present and up to date.
The function @code{MC_(instrument)} handles the instrumentation of calls for
DynComp.
It is primarily contained in a switch statement; each case handles one
instruction type, and the body contains both the original code for
Memcheck and also the code for DynComp. After the update, you should
double-check that each clause contains corresponding code: any changes
to the Memcheck versions are reflected in the DynComp versions, and
any new clause has a DynComp version.


@node Updating Fjalar/Kvasir
@section Updating Fjalar/Kvasir

Like @file{fjalar/mc_translate.c},
@file{fjalar/kvasir/dyncomp_translate.c}
is derived from
@file{valgrind/memcheck/mc_translate.c}.  However, due to extensive
modifications and appending @quoteleft{}@code{_DC}@quoteright{} to most
function names to avoid
conflicts, merging the changes to @file{mc_translate.c} into it
automatically is of little use.
Instead, you will need to use the changes to @file{mc_translate.c}, which
this command shows, as a template for
editing @file{fjalar/kvasir/dyncomp_translate.c} by hand:

@smallexample
git -C $INV/fjalar diff valgrind-merge/base valgrind-merge/new -- valgrind/fjalar/mc_translate.c
@end smallexample

Additionally Fjalar/Kvasir need to be updated to properly handle any
changes in Valgrind API/functionality. For the most part Valgrind
maintains a relatively stable interface to its tools. Any tool-visible
changes should be noted in the change logs.

If the build fails in the rule for
@file{fjalar_core_decls.stamp}, then a core declaration has changed.
Update the copy in @file{valgrind/fjalar/fjalar_core_decls.h} to match
the core header that it names.

A somewhat more problematic area are changes in the VEX IR. DynComp
makes heavy use of the VEX IR, so any changes in it need to be
reflected. Most of the changes to the public VEX IR can be discovered
by running the following command:

@smallexample
git -C $INV/fjalar diff -b valgrind-merge/base valgrind-merge/new -- valgrind/VEX/pub/libvex_ir.h
@end smallexample

It is also worth noting that almost all the changes will be for machines
that we do not support.

The files with all the VEX IR code for DynComp are located in
@file{dyncomp_translate.[ch]}. @file{dyncomp_translate.c} is structured primarily
into functions of the form @code{expr2tags_[EXPRESSION_TYPE]()}.
These functions will contain a switch for all VEX IR instructions
corresponding to the expression type and some call to a DynComp tag
function. Most often VEX IR changes will be syntactical in nature and
will only involve changing the names of the instructions. If the log
indicates that new VEX IR instructions have been added, they will need
to be explicitly supported by DynComp.
@xref{Supporting new VEX IR Instruction}, for
guidelines on supporting new VEX instructions.


@node Get New Valgrind Regtest Baseline Results
@section Get New Valgrind Regtest Baseline Results

Valgrind has a set of verification tests known as @command{regtest}.  We want
to run these tests on a clean version of Valgrind to establish a
baseline set of results for partial verification of the merge process.
Because the tests run in a copy of @file{valgrind-new} (see
below), you can run them at any time after you have obtained
@file{valgrind-new}, for example while you merge the sources.

You will need to make sure @command{gdb} is installed on your machine
to run these tests. Also, they are very sensitive
to the system software on the host machine.  In most cases, there will be
some failures. Rather, it is important to capture the results
as they should be similar after the source merge. They may not be
identical due to Valgrind improvements or the addition of new test cases.

To run the @command{regtest} verification tests, run
@command{upstream-regtest.sh}, giving it the file to write the list of
failing tests to:

@example
$INV/fjalar/upstream-regtest.sh $INV/regtest-upstream-$VNEWTAG.txt
@end example

@noindent
The script checks that @file{valgrind-new} is at @code{$VNEWTAG}, which
must be set.  It builds a copy of @file{valgrind-new}, in
@file{$INV/valgrind-new-build}, and runs the tests there.  It never builds
in @file{valgrind-new} itself, so that @file{valgrind-new} stays a clean
clone, which becomes @file{valgrind-old} for the next merge.

@noindent
This will take about 10-15 minutes.  The test output is in
@file{$INV/valgrind-new-build/test.log}; a summary of the @command{regtest}
results is at the end of it.  @file{$INV/regtest-upstream-$VNEWTAG.txt} lists the
failing tests, in the expected-failures format that
@command{check-regtest.sh} describes.  We will use these results in
@ref{Testing Valgrind}.  Keep @file{valgrind-new-build} until the merge
has landed (@pxref{Testing Valgrind}).  @ref{Testing Valgrind} also
compares the failures after the merge with the failures in Fjalar's tree
before the merge, which you saved in
@ref{Verify current sources pass the regression tests}.

