@node Updating Readelf from Binutils
@chapter Updating Readelf from Binutils

@menu
* Overview::
* Get Current Valgrind Regtest Baseline Results::
* Merging the binutils changes::
* Hints to modifying dwarf.c and readelf.c::
@end menu


@node Overview
@section Overview

The GNU Binutils are completely independent of Valgrind and have their
own home page (@uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/binutils/})
and release site (@uref{https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/binutils/}).
While the majority of this document discusses how to update Valgrind,
this chapter describes how to update the @command{readelf} code from Binutils.
For ease of verification, it is recommended that you do not update Valgrind
and Binutils at the same time.


GNU Binutils is a collection of approximately twenty binary tools.
We are interested only in @command{readelf}, a tool that displays
information from an ELF format object file.  Fjalar does not use this
binary directly, rather a portion of the source code for Fjalar contains
a modified version of the source used to build @command{readelf}.


@node Get Current Valgrind Regtest Baseline Results
@section Get Current Valgrind Regtest Baseline Results

Just as is done for a Valgrind source update
(@ref{Get New Valgrind Regtest Baseline Results}),
we want to run the Valgrind @command{regtest} regression tests to
establish a baseline set of results for partial verification of the
@command{readelf} update process.  However, these tests use the current
Fjalar sources rather than upstream's, so run them prior to the source
update.  (A Valgrind update runs them prior to the merge too;
@pxref{Verify current sources pass the regression tests}.)

@smallexample
if [ -f $INV/fjalar/valgrind/Makefile ]; then make -C $INV/fjalar clean; fi \
  && (set -o pipefail; make -C $INV/fjalar build 2>&1 | tee $INV/fjalar/build-before.log) \
  && $INV/fjalar/check-regtest.sh
@end smallexample

@noindent
@file{build-before.log} is for comparing compiler warnings after the
merge (@pxref{Hints to modifying dwarf.c and readelf.c}).
The @code{clean} makes the build recompile every file, so that the log
contains all the warnings.  It is skipped in a tree that has never been
built, where it would fail because @command{make build} has not yet
created @file{valgrind/Makefile}.

@noindent
As noted in @ref{Get New Valgrind Regtest Baseline Results},
we do not expect all the tests to pass.  @command{check-regtest.sh}
compares the failures with the expected failures for this operating
system, in @file{regtest-expected/<os>.txt}, and must report no
unexpected failures.  If this operating system has no such file, create
a local one, which you should not commit:

@smallexample
$INV/fjalar/check-regtest.sh --log $INV/fjalar/regtest-<os>.log --update
@end smallexample

@noindent
@ref{Testing Valgrind} compares against it.


@node Merging the binutils changes
@section Merging the binutils changes

Before merging, run Daikon's Kvasir tests:

@example
  make -C $DAIKONDIR/tests/kvasir-tests clean-all
  make -C $DAIKONDIR/tests/kvasir-tests regression-tests
@end example

@noindent
(Make sure that @file{$DAIKONDIR/fjalar} is the Fjalar you are updating;
@pxref{Verify current sources pass the regression tests}.)

@noindent
@xref{Verify current sources pass the regression tests} for the expected
results of running these tests (but don't follow any of the other
instructions in that section).

Set @env{BUNEWVER} to the Binutils version to merge; find the latest one
at @uref{https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/binutils/?C=M;O=D}.  The old version is
the one in @file{$INV/fjalar/valgrind/REVISION}.

@example
export BUNEWVER=2.42
@end example

To see how hard the merge will be without doing it, pass @option{--trial}:

@example
$INV/fjalar/merge-binutils.sh --trial $BUNEWVER
@end example

@noindent
For each file of the old release that Fjalar changed, it prints the number
of changes (runs of consecutive changed lines), added lines, and removed
lines in Fjalar's version.  Then it prints the number of conflicts that the
merge would have in each file.  It merges in memory, so it changes neither
the tree, the index, nor any ref, and it allows uncommitted changes, which
it includes in the measurement.  It requires Git 2.40 or later, or 2.43 or
later with a cherry-pick option.  With @option{--trial}, the only
cherry-pick options are strategy options, such as @option{-Xpatience}.  If
the merge would have too many conflicts to resolve by hand, merge an
intermediate Binutils release first, as described below.

Then merge:

@example
$INV/fjalar/merge-binutils.sh $BUNEWVER
@end example

@noindent
The script merges into the Fjalar tree that contains it, so run the copy
in the tree that you are merging into.  It requires that the tree has no
uncommitted changes, so that this command undoes the merge:

@example
git -C $INV/fjalar reset --hard
@end example

The script works in @file{/tmp/$USER/binutils/}.  It downloads the old and
new Binutils releases, verifies their signatures, and extracts the parts
that it needs.  Then it creates two commits, which contain only the files
of each release that Fjalar has a copy of, at the paths of Fjalar's
copies:  @code{binutils-merge/base} for the old release, and
@code{binutils-merge/new} for the new one.  Fjalar's copies of files from
Binutils' @file{binutils/} directory, such as @file{dwarf.c} and
@file{readelf.c}, are in @file{valgrind/fjalar/}, and its copies of files
from @file{include/} and @file{libiberty/} are in
@file{valgrind/fjalar/include/} and @file{valgrind/fjalar/libiberty/}.
Then the script runs @command{git cherry-pick} of
@code{binutils-merge/new}.  That is a 3-way merge:  it compares the Fjalar
version of each file, the old Binutils version, and the new Binutils
version.  It applies a change even if Fjalar has changed the lines around
it, and it leaves conflict markers in a file where both Binutils and
Fjalar changed the same lines.  Unlike @command{patch}, it uses no fuzz,
so it is less likely to apply a change at the wrong location.  It still
can, for example among repeated lines such as the cases of a
@code{switch} statement.  The script stages the result of the merge, but
does not commit it.

This command shows what the Binutils developers changed:

@example
git -C $INV/fjalar diff binutils-merge/base binutils-merge/new
@end example

Before it merges, the script writes Fjalar's changes to the old release to
@file{/tmp/$USER/binutils/@var{oldver}-@var{newver}/binutils-Fjalar.diff},
such as @file{/tmp/$USER/binutils/2.39-2.42/binutils-Fjalar.diff}.  It is a
checklist for verifying that the merge kept each of Fjalar's changes.  A
conflict shows Fjalar's side, but a merge can lose a change without
reporting a conflict:  for example, if upstream moved or rewrote the code,
the new copy lacks Fjalar's @code{#if 0} or @code{printf} guard.  For each
hunk in the diff, find the corresponding code in the merged file and check
that it still has Fjalar's change.

If the conflicts are too many to merge by hand, undo the merge and merge
an intermediate Binutils release first.  Complete that merge, including
updating @code{BINUTILS_VERSION} in @file{$INV/fjalar/valgrind/REVISION}
(see below), and commit it before you merge the next release.  The script
takes the old version from that line, and it requires that the tree has no
uncommitted changes.

Now, handle the conflicts.  @file{merge-binutils.sh} lists the files with
conflicts, and @command{git status} lists them as unmerged.  Each conflict
in a file is marked by @samp{<<<<<<<}, @samp{|||||||}, @samp{=======}, and
@samp{>>>>>>>} lines, which surround Fjalar's version, the old Binutils
version, and the new Binutils version.  (The script uses this conflict
style regardless of your Git configuration's @code{merge.conflictStyle}.)
Mark each file as resolved with @command{git add}.  When you are done, this
command produces no output:

@example
git -C $INV/fjalar diff --name-only --diff-filter=U
@end example

@noindent
Before compiling, make sure that no conflict markers remain.

The include files merge with few, if any, conflicts; @file{dwarf.c} and
@file{readelf.c} will probably have many.  As you edit the new files, you
may wish to review the diffs in the previous versions as the new diffs
should be similar.  @xref{Hints to modifying dwarf.c and readelf.c} for
more source details that may help you with the merge process.

A visual merge tool can make this process easier.  @command{git mergetool}
starts one on each file that has conflicts, with Fjalar's version, the old
Binutils version, and the new Binutils version.  For example, to use
@command{meld}:

@example
git -C $INV/fjalar mergetool --tool=meld valgrind/fjalar/dwarf.c
@end example

The new Binutils code may need things that Fjalar does not have:

@itemize
@item
New header files.  @file{merge-binutils.sh} lists the headers that the new
release's copies of Fjalar's files include but the old release's do not,
directly or through another such header, and that Fjalar does not have.
The new release's copies include the headers in
@file{valgrind/fjalar/include/} and @file{valgrind/fjalar/libiberty/},
so the script scans those too.  The merge does not add the missing
headers, because it merges only files that Fjalar already has.  Copy the
ones that are needed from the new Binutils tree,
@file{/tmp/$USER/binutils/binutils-$BUNEWVER/} (call it @var{bunew}).
A header from @file{@var{bunew}/include/} or
@file{@var{bunew}/libiberty/} keeps its location relative to
@var{bunew}:  for example, a new file in
@file{@var{bunew}/include/elf/} goes in
@file{$INV/fjalar/valgrind/fjalar/include/elf/}.  A header from any
other directory, such as @file{@var{bunew}/binutils/} (like
@file{bucomm.h}) or @file{@var{bunew}/bfd/} (like @file{elf-attrs.h}),
goes in @file{$INV/fjalar/valgrind/fjalar/} itself; also add its path
in @var{bunew}, such as @file{bfd/elf-attrs.h}, to @code{BINUTILS_FILES}
in @file{merge-binutils.sh}.  Add each copy with @command{git add}.
Then later merges merge it too.

The script separately lists the new headers that it does not find in the
directories that it searches, but that have the same name as files
elsewhere in the release, such as in @file{@var{bunew}/opcodes/} or
@file{@var{bunew}/zlib/}; it lists those files.  Copy the ones that are
needed as described above.  It also lists the new headers that are not in
the new release at all.  They are system headers, such as @file{zstd.h}, or
headers that building Binutils generates, such as @file{bfdver.h}.
Fjalar does not build Binutils, so make sure that it does not need a
generated header, or define what the new code uses from it.
@item
Deleted files.  The script lists the files that Fjalar has a copy of but
that the new release lacks.  The merge deletes Fjalar's copy, or reports
a modify/delete conflict if Fjalar changed it.  Make sure that no Fjalar
code still includes or uses such a file, and remove it from
@code{BINUTILS_FILES} if it is listed there.
@item
Code for features that Fjalar does not use, such as SFrame
(@samp{readelf --sframe}, added in Binutils 2.40, which needs
@file{libsframe}).  Disable such code with
@code{#if 0 // This code is not needed for Fjalar.}, as was done for CTF,
rather than adding the libraries it needs.
@item
Support for zstd-compressed debug sections (Binutils 2.40 and later):
make sure that it compiles away when @code{HAVE_ZSTD} is not defined.
@item
Definitions from Binutils headers that Fjalar replaces:  the copies
include Fjalar's own @file{sysdep.h}, @file{bfd.h}, @file{libiberty.h},
and a few other headers, in @file{valgrind/fjalar/binutils-shim/}, rather
than Binutils' or the system's.  Each defines only what the copies use.
If the new code uses a definition that one of them lacks, compilation
fails; add the definition, taken from the same Binutils release as the new
code.  (Binutils' @file{bfd.h} is generated from @file{bfd/bfd-in2.h}.)
@end itemize

After you have resolved the conflicts, check that no call of a Fjalar
hook was lost or duplicated.  The hooks are the functions declared in
@file{typedata.h}, @file{fjalar_dwarf.h}, @file{fjalar_include.h},
@file{fjalar_main.h}, and @file{GenericHashtable.h}, which build Fjalar's
DWARF entries, symbol tables, and line-number table, either before the merge
or now.  This command
prints each hook whose number of calls in @file{dwarf.c} or
@file{readelf.c} changed, outside @code{#if 0} regions and comments:

@example
$INV/fjalar/check-hook-counts.sh
@end example

@noindent
Explain each difference.  The script's header comment gives the details.

After you have completed the source merge,
update the ``@code{export BINUTILS_VERSION=}'' line in @file{fjalar/valgrind/REVISION}.
Then proceed to @ref{Compiling and Testing}.


@need 2000
@node Hints to modifying dwarf.c and readelf.c
@section Hints to modifying dwarf.c and readelf.c

Many sections of code in @file{dwarf.c} and @code{readelf.c}, some
of them quite large, are not needed for Fjalar and have been
disabled with C preprocessor commands.  The general format is:

@example
#if 0 // This code is not needed for Fjalar.

...  <unused code> ...

#endif // This code is not needed for Fjalar.
@end example

@noindent
Keeping the unused code instead of deleting it makes the
merge process go much smoother with fewer
conflicts.  It is unnecessary (and inadvisable) to make any
modifications to these disabled sections of code.

One complication is that Valgrind is compiled with many more
compiler warning options than Binutils.  Currently, this list is:
@example
-Wcast-align
-Wcast-qual
-Wempty-body
-Wformat
-Wformat-security
-Wformat-signedness
-Wignored-qualifiers
-Wimplicit-fallthrough=2
-Wlogical-op
-Wmissing-declarations
-Wmissing-parameter-type
-Wno-cast-qual
-Wold-style-declaration
-Wpointer-arith
-Wwrite-strings
@end example
This can cause a very large number of warnings from Binutils code
that has been merged into Valgrind.  Most are benign, but you will have to
inspect them all.  To list the new ones, save the output of the build
before the merge, and of a build after the merge that recompiles the
Binutils-derived files, and compare them:

@smallexample
make -C $INV/fjalar/valgrind/fjalar clean
(set -o pipefail; make -C $INV/fjalar/valgrind/fjalar 2>&1 | tee $INV/fjalar/build-after.log)
$INV/fjalar/compare-warnings.sh --binutils $INV/fjalar/build-before.log $INV/fjalar/build-after.log
@end smallexample

@noindent
(@ref{Get Current Valgrind Regtest Baseline Results} saves
@file{build-before.log}.)  Do not edit a copy of a Binutils file to remove a
warning, because each change to a copy makes subsequent merges harder.
Instead, disable the warning for the copies:  with a @code{#pragma GCC
diagnostic} in @file{valgrind/fjalar/binutils-shim/sysdep.h}, or, for a
warning that a pragma cannot disable, such as @option{-Wformat-signedness},
in @file{valgrind/fjalar/fjalar.am}.  Do fix a warning that reveals a bug.

A Valgrind tool cannot call the C runtime library.  Fjalar's
@file{sysdep.h}, in @file{valgrind/fjalar/binutils-shim/}, which the
copies of Binutils files include first, maps each C library function that
they call, such as @code{malloc} or @code{strlen}, to its Valgrind
equivalent, such as @code{VG_(malloc)} or @code{VG_(strlen)}.  Fjalar's
@file{assert.h}, in the same directory, maps @code{assert} to
@code{tl_assert}.  So do not edit a call of a C library function in new
code.  If new code calls a C library function that @file{sysdep.h} does
not map and that @file{my_libc.c} does not define, the compiler reports an
"implicit declaration" error (GCC before version 14 reports only a
warning) or, if some header declares the function, the linker reports an
"undefined reference" error; then add a macro for
the function to @file{sysdep.h}, or, if Valgrind has no equivalent, define
the function in @file{my_libc.c}.

The other major area of difficulty when doing the source merge
is with the @code{printf} calls
found in the source.  Remember that the original @command{readelf}
is a tool to print out information about binary object files.
Fjalar uses this code to disassemble the object files and harvest
information needed for analysis of the user's program. Fjalar does
not want to print out this information (unless a debugging
option has been selected).  Thus, almost all the
calls to @code{printf} or @code{putchar} need to be guarded by
a test of one of the Fjalar debug options.  In particular,
@code{fjalar_debug} for the output of debugging information
and @code{fjalar_debug_dump} for the output of the contents of
the binary file being processed.

@need 2000
Please note that these @code{if} guards on the @code{printf} statements
lead to a
subtle problem; if the operands of the @code{printf} call have
side effects, then they must be removed from the @code{printf}
and the value assigned to a temp.

@noindent
For example:
@example
printf ("DW_OP_bit_piece: ");
printf (_("size: %s "),
          dwarf_vmatoa ("u", read_uleb128 (data, &bytes_read, end)));
data += bytes_read;
@end example
@noindent
needs to be rewritten as:
@example
uconst_data = read_uleb128 (data, &bytes_read, end);
if (fjalar_debug_dump)
printf ("DW_OP_bit_piece: ");
if (fjalar_debug_dump)
printf (_("size: %s "),
          dwarf_vmatoa ("u", uconst_data));
data += bytes_read;
@end example

A couple of preprocessor short cuts have been used to help with the process.
@example
if (fjalar_debug) printf (<print data>);
@end example
@noindent
may be replaced with:
@example
FJALAR_DPRINTF(<print data>);
@end example
@noindent
This shortcut is used extensively in @file{readelf.c}.

Within @file{dwarf.c} there is a large section of code
(several thousand lines) that is delimited by the following:
@example
#define printf(...) do @{if(ok_to_print) printf(__VA_ARGS__);@} while (0)
...
#undef printf
@end example
@noindent
This allows the @code{printf} statements within this section of code
to be used without modification.

TODO:  This trick could be applied elsewhere.  For instance,  
the routine @code{display_loc_list} in @file{dwarf.c} could use the #define trick (for 3 output
routines, not just @code{printf}).

A missing @code{printf} guard should not be a big problem.  Typically, the
Fjalar tool will operate as expected but contain superfluous information
in its output.  This will likely be caught as a diff in a @file{.out}
file when running the kvasir-tests regressions.

To learn why upstream changed some code, consult the Binutils history.
Its tags have the form @code{binutils-2_42}.  For example:
@example
git clone --filter=blob:none https://sourceware.org/git/binutils-gdb.git
git -C binutils-gdb log binutils-2_39..binutils-2_42 -- binutils/dwarf.c
@end example


@node Compiling and Testing
@chapter Compiling and Testing

We must now ensure that the merged code compiles correctly and passes
the regression test suite.

@menu
* Compiling Fjalar::
* Testing Valgrind::
* Testing Fjalar::
* Documenting Changes::
@end menu


@node Compiling Fjalar
@section Compiling Fjalar

The first step is to build Fjalar/Kvasir.

@example
make -C $INV/fjalar build
@end example

The build target should @command{autogen} the @file{config} and @file{Makefiles}
and compile Fjalar. Fix any compilation errors.

Once you have run @command{make build} once, thereafter you can just run
@command{make -C $INV/fjalar/valgrind/fjalar install} (or
@command{make -C $INV/fjalar/valgrind install}, if you changed files
outside @file{fjalar/}).  Do not omit @code{install}:  the Kvasir tests
run the installed Fjalar under @file{valgrind/inst}, so without
@code{install} they silently test the previous build.


@node Testing Valgrind
@section Testing Valgrind

Before we test Fjalar, it is a good idea to verify we haven't
broken the underlying Valgrind.  @command{check-regtest.sh} runs
Valgrind's @command{regtest} tests and compares the failures with the
expected failures for this operating system, in
@file{regtest-expected/<os>.txt}:

@smallexample
$INV/fjalar/check-regtest.sh
@end smallexample

@noindent
For a Binutils update, which does not change Valgrind, it must report no
unexpected failures.  A new failure is a bug in the merge, or the result
of an operating system or compiler update; to tell which, run the tests
on the old sources.

For a Valgrind update, the expected failures change, because the new
release adds, removes, and fixes tests.  First compare the failures with
the list that @command{upstream-regtest.sh} wrote
(@pxref{Get New Valgrind Regtest Baseline Results}):

@smallexample
$INV/fjalar/check-regtest.sh --log $INV/fjalar/regtest-<os>.log \
  --expected $INV/regtest-upstream-$VNEWTAG.txt --report-only
@end smallexample

@noindent
Each failure that it reports is not an upstream failure.  If it is in
@file{$INV/regtest-before-merge.txt}
(@pxref{Verify current sources pass the regression tests}), it failed
before the merge too, and is specific to Fjalar; otherwise it was probably
caused by the merge, so fix it.  A test that
@file{regtest-expected/<os>.txt} marks with @samp{?} may fail only
sometimes, so if such a test is not in
@file{regtest-before-merge.txt}, run the tests again before you conclude
that the merge broke it.  Only when no failure of the second kind remains, rewrite the
expected failures, which adds the new upstream failures and removes the
tests that now pass, and review the diff:

@smallexample
$INV/fjalar/check-regtest.sh --log $INV/fjalar/regtest-<os>.log --update
@end smallexample

@noindent
Commit the diff only if the list is committed, not if you created it
locally.

For a Valgrind update, keep the build copy until the merge has landed,
because a failure that appears only in CI may need to be compared with
upstream's results; then delete it and the list of failures before the
merge:
@code{rm -rf $INV/valgrind-new-build $INV/regtest-before-merge.txt}.


@node Testing Fjalar
@section Testing Fjalar

The regression suite is located in the tests/kvasir-tests directory
of the Daikon tree.
It can be run using the following commands (takes about 20 minutes):

@example
make -C $DAIKONDIR/tests/kvasir-tests clean-all
make -C $DAIKONDIR/tests/kvasir-tests regression-tests
@end example

@noindent
(Make sure that @file{$DAIKONDIR/fjalar} is the Fjalar you merged into;
@pxref{Verify current sources pass the regression tests}.)

@noindent
@xref{Verify current sources pass the regression tests} for the expected
results of running these tests.

If tests fail with differences that contain "<STACK_ADDR>", "<STATIC_ADDR>" or
"<HEAP_ADDR>" on the goal file side and hexadecimal values on the new file
side, it might be due to compiler changes or platform changes that
caused code or data to reside at different memory addresses.  Compare the
address ranges in @file{$DAIKONDIR/tests/kvasir-tests/clean-output.pl}
with those in the new binaries and see if changes to
@file{clean-output.pl} are required.

After a Binutils merge, also run Kvasir with @option{--fjalar-debug-dump},
which mimics @samp{readelf --debug-dump}, on a program compiled with
@option{-gdwarf-5}, and compare its output with that of the new Binutils'
@command{readelf}.  The system's @command{readelf} may be older, so build
the new one; @command{build-binutils-readelf.sh} builds it and prints its
path:

@example
READELF=$($INV/fjalar/build-binutils-readelf.sh $BUNEWVER)
$READELF --debug-dump @var{program}
@end example

The Kvasir tests check only the @file{.decls} and @file{.dtrace} files, not
the output of @option{--fjalar-debug-dump} and @option{--fjalar-debug}.
@command{compare-debug-output.sh} checks all of them: it runs Fjalar on
programs compiled from Fjalar's tests and compares the output with a
baseline.  Its first run saves the baseline, and later runs compare with
it.  Save the baseline with a built Fjalar tree that does not contain the
merge, such as a clone of the master branch, and then compare with the
merged tree:

@example
@var{unmerged-fjalar}/compare-debug-output.sh /tmp/$USER/debug-output
$INV/fjalar/compare-debug-output.sh /tmp/$USER/debug-output
@end example

@noindent
A Valgrind or Binutils merge may change the output intentionally; review
each difference.  See the comments at the top of
@file{compare-debug-output.sh} for details.


@node Documenting Changes
@section Documenting Changes

Any user-visible changes should be documented in
@file{$DAIKONDIR/doc/CHANGELOG.md}.
Additionally, the first line in @file{fjalar/valgrind/REVISION} should be updated
with the Valgrind version used for this merge.

Do not edit @file{valgrind/.gitignore}.
Files that only Fjalar's changes generate should be added to Fjalar's
own @file{.gitignore}, at the top of the Fjalar tree.

After building and
running the tests, this command lists the untracked files in test
directories that neither file matches:

@example
git -C $INV/fjalar ls-files --others --exclude-per-directory=.gitignore -- '*tests/*'
@end example

@noindent
Review the list: it may also include files that should be
committed rather than ignored.


@node Committing
@chapter Committing

Once the test suite passes without errors and all changes are documented,
the changes should be committed to the version control repository.

@menu
* Commit commands::
@end menu


@node Commit commands
@section Commit commands

Next, we do a final visual verification of the changes and commit them
to the repository.

@smallexample
# Double-check the diffs, including those of the commits made during the
# merge - standard practice before committing.  Comparing with the
# commit where the merge branched from codespecs/fjalar works even if
# origin is a fork.
git -C $INV/fjalar fetch https://github.com/codespecs/fjalar.git master
git -C $INV/fjalar diff -b "$(git -C $INV/fjalar merge-base FETCH_HEAD HEAD)" | more
# If looks OK, proceed with commit.
git -C $INV/fjalar commit -a -m "Valgrind merge from $@{VOLDTAG@} to $@{VNEWTAG@}"
@end smallexample

Note: We recommend saving Fjalar's changes to the base Valgrind
(@pxref{Merging Valgrind}) to further document the process.


@node Valgrind modifications
@appendix Valgrind modifications

In an effort to aid in determining the appropriate measures to take
when merging conflicted files, this section briefly explains our
modifications to Valgrind files.

The most important set of changes is the addition of extra shadow
state in Coregrind and VEX. The shadow area is an area of memory that
Valgrind provides for tools to use. Unfortunately, it is of a fixed
size, and Memcheck uses all of it. We've had to increase the size of
the shadow area.
Additionally, we had to modify some of the VEX architecture files to
include additional information specific to the amd64 and x86 platforms.
Finally, we had to extend Valgrind's implementation of the C library
with a few extra functions.

To see all of Fjalar's modifications to the Valgrind release that
@file{valgrind/REVISION} names, run:

@example
$INV/fjalar/diff-from-valgrind.sh
@end example

@noindent
It accepts the options of @command{git diff}, such as @option{--stat} and
@option{-w}.  It compares with @file{$INV/valgrind-old}; to compare with
another clone of that release, such as @file{$INV/valgrind-new} after a
merge, give @option{--valgrind=@var{dir}}.  Every modification is a
potential conflict in the next merge, so keep them few:  put Fjalar's code
in Fjalar's own files where possible, and keep upstream's whitespace.
Continuous integration runs @command{check-valgrind-whitespace.sh}, which
fails if Fjalar's copy of an upstream file has a change of whitespace
alone, such as removed trailing whitespace or an added blank line.  On a line
whose text Fjalar also changed, a change of whitespace adds no conflict, so
the check ignores it.

(Remember that we assume @env{$INV} points to a parent directory that
contains both the Daikon and Fjalar source trees.)

@table @asis
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/configure.ac
Add fjalar/Makefile to list of targets
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/coregrind/m_libcbase.c
Add VG_ version of strnlen
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/coregrind/m_libcfile.c
Add VG_ version of mkdir
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/coregrind/m_machine.c
Add get and set functions for additional machine registers
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/coregrind/m_scheduler/scheduler.c
Modify an assertion that triggers based on shadow size
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/coregrind/pub_core_machine.h
Add additional general purpose registers to the guest state
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/coregrind/pub_core_threadstate.h
The declaration of the extra shadow space
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/include/pub_tool_libcbase.h
Add VG_ version of strnlen
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/include/pub_tool_libcfile.h
Add VG_ version of mkdir and fcntl
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/include/pub_tool_machine.h
Add get and set functions for additional machine registers
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/Makefile.am
Add fjalar to the list of tools
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/none/tests/Makefile.am
Add @nospellcheck{coregrind/libcoregrind-amd64-linux.a to libvex} tests
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/tests/check_headers_and_includes
Add fjalar to @nospellcheck{tool_dirs and inst to dirs_to_ignore}
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/VEX/priv/host_amd64_defs.c
