@node Memcheck modifications
@appendix Memcheck modifications

The modifications made to Memcheck are more organizational in nature.
Fjalar code that uses static functions of @file{mc_main.c} or
@file{mc_translate.c} is in a file that each of them includes, and
@file{mc_fjalar.h} and @file{mc_translate.h} declare that code for the
rest of Fjalar.  Several changes to remove compile warnings
for use of function name as variable.

Other modifications include:

@table @asis
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/fjalar/mc_main.c
Run-time arguments for Fjalar.  (Fjalar does not change Memcheck's
details, such as its name and version, in @code{mc_pre_clo_init}; instead,
@code{fjalar_pre_clo_init} in @file{fjalar_main.c} and each tool's
@code{fjalar_tool_pre_clo_init} override them.)  Also, an @code{#include} of
@file{fjalar_mc_main.h}, which defines the functions that Fjalar adds to
Memcheck (@file{mc_fjalar.h} declares them).  Those functions call
static functions of @file{mc_main.c}, such as @code{set_vabits2},
@code{set_sec_vbits8}, @code{get_vabits2},
@code{is_mem_defined}, and @code{is_mem_addressable}, so the
@code{#include} must follow the definitions of those functions.
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/fjalar/mc_translate.c
In Memcheck's instrumentation block, duplicate each case for DynComp.
Also, a @code{DC} kind of @code{TempKind}, for DynComp's temporaries,
and an @code{#include} of @file{fjalar_mc_translate.h}, which defines
the functions through which the rest of Fjalar uses Memcheck's
instrumenter (@file{mc_translate.h} declares them).  Those functions
use @code{MCEnv} and static functions of @file{mc_translate.c}, such as
@code{stmt}, @code{newTemp}, and @code{shadowTypeV}, so the
@code{#include} must follow the definitions of those.
@end table


@node Supporting new VEX IR Instruction
@appendix Supporting new VEX IR Instruction

It is recommended that you acquaint yourself with the VEX IR by reading
through:

@table @asis
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/VEX/pub/libvex.h
@item $INV/fjalar/valgrind/VEX/pub/libvex_ir.h
@end table

In addition to being the primary headers for the VEX library, the above
files represent the majority of the public documentation on VEX.
Valgrind's translation pipeline consists of the following:

@smallexample
Native assembly -> Pre-instrumented VEX IR -> Post-Instrumented VEX IR -> Final assembly
@end smallexample

Valgrind begins by translating the entirety of an assembly basic block
into VEX IR. Valgrind then allows tools to examine the
translated basic block and insert their own instrumentation. Valgrind
finishes by translating the instrumented IR back into the machine's native
assembly.

In order to keep track of comparabilities, DynComp instruments almost
every VEX IR instruction type. Any added instructions will likely need
to be supported by DynComp. In general DynComp's functionality parallels
Memcheck's, so the best starting point for implementing support for a
new instruction would be to mirror Memcheck's implementation. DynComp's
layout is very similar to Memcheck's, so mirroring functionality should
be fairly straightforward.

New VEX IR operations (@code{Iop_*} values) are added in many Valgrind
releases.  @code{expr2tags_Unop_DC}, @code{expr2tags_Binop_DC},
@code{expr2tags_Triop_DC}, and @code{expr2tags_Qop_DC} panic when they
encounter an operation that they do not handle.  So, every new operation
must be considered, even if the regression tests do not exercise it.

The test @file{valgrind/fjalar/tests/dyncomp-op-coverage-test.sh}, which
@code{make fjalar-test} runs, finds the operations to consider.  It takes
each operation's arity from @code{typeOfPrimop} in
@file{VEX/priv/ir_defs.c}, and it checks that each operation either has a
case label in the function for its arity or is listed in the comment of
@file{dyncomp_translate.c} that begins ``The following opcodes are not
implemented'', in the subsection for that function.  The comment lists the
operations that the supported guests, amd64 and x86, do not use, so the
test also checks that the amd64 and x86 front ends
(@file{VEX/priv/guest_amd64_*.c}, @file{VEX/priv/guest_x86_*.c}, and
@file{VEX/priv/guest_generic_*.c}) use none of them.  Finally, it checks
that no listed operation also has a case label, that every listed
operation still exists and has the arity of its subsection, and that no
case label falls through to the @code{default} case.

When the test fails after a merge, give each operation that it reports a
case label in the function for its arity, with the same tag behavior as
similar operations (Memcheck's treatment in
@file{memcheck/mc_translate.c} is a good guide).  If no supported guest
uses the operation, you may instead list it in the comment.  Remove a
listed operation that VEX no longer has.

To find the unhandled operations that a program reaches without stopping
at the first one, run it with @option{--dyncomp-unhandled-ops=warn}, which
prints a warning the first time that DynComp encounters each unhandled
operation and approximates its tag behavior.

Another type of addition that will need to be supported are added
"IR Expressions." Most VEX IR instructions are implemented as a set
of IR Expressions - Take the following IR instructions for example:

@example
t5 = Add32(t12,0x8:I32)
t10 = CmpLE32S(t2,0x21:I32)
@end example

The above IR Instructions are of the type WrTmp and they store the
result of an IR Expression into a temporary. These instructions consist
of the destination temporary, an IR Expression, which conceptually is
the operation to be formed, and the arguments to that expression. Most
IR Instructions will have a similar format. DynComp is particularly
interested in analyzing all possible IR Expressions.

@example
$INV/fjalar/valgrind/fjalar/kvasir/dyncomp_translate.c
@end example

contains the following set of functions for processing IR Expressions.

@table @code
@item @strong{IRExpr* expr2tags_DC ( DCEnv* dce, IRExpr* e )}
The main expression handling function. Contains a switch which is
responsible for delegating expression handling to one of the other functions
@item @strong{IRAtom* expr2tags_Qop_DC(...)}
Responsible for the handling of expressions which take 4 arguments.
@item @strong{IRAtom* expr2tags_Triop_DC(...)}
Responsible for the handling of expressions which take 3 arguments.
@item @strong{IRAtom* expr2tags_Binop_DC(...)}
Responsible for the handling of expressions which take 2 arguments.
@item @strong{IRExpr* expr2tags_Unop_DC(...)}
Responsible for the handling of expressions which take 1 argument.
@item @strong{IRAtom* expr2tags_LDle_DC(...)}
Responsible for the handling of load expressions.
@item @strong{IRAtom* handleCCall_DC(...)}
Responsible for the handling of calls to pure functions.
@item @strong{IRAtom* expr2tags_ITE_DC(...)}
Responsible for the handling of if-then-else expressions.
@end table

If a new IR Expression is added, it will need to be handled by one of
the above functions. The easiest way to implement it will be to base it
on the implementation of an existing instruction. Alternatively, it
should be straightforward to mimic Memcheck's handling of the expression.

